A||||1||james||sticking tachometer
Z||000000||james||02-16-99||09:56 PM||||I was wondering why all fieros i've ever seen have a tachometer that sticks when the engine is off and I was wondering if this would make the reading any less accurate
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||02-19-99||04:15 PM||||That's because all (original) Fiero gauges are so called "air core meters". Normal gauges have a needle connected to a spring which forces them to a "0" position. The needle is attached to a coil around a magnet. When a current is applied to the coil, it causes the needle to move (the magnetic field generated by the coil attracts or repels the magnet). When the current disappears, the needle is pulled back to zero.<p>An air core meter uses two coils in an angle of 90 degrees. Each coil produces its own magnetic field resulting in a "magnetic vector". A magnet (at the base of the needle) placed in the middle will try to align its north- and southpole to this magnetic vector (like a compass) and will turn until it has done so.<p>The advantage over an ordinary gauge is that the needle is able to make full 360 degree turns and actually keep on turning. Also, you don't have to worry about the spring getting weaker over time, causing erratic readings.<p>When you turn off the ignition, the needle just stays where it is, because it has no spring attached to it forcing it to a zero position. Because the needle is quite hard to turn, it will not drop and point straight down.<p>The speedometer works exactly the same way but because most of the time you are standing still when you turn off the ignition, you don't notice it doesn't return...  ;)
